{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Maier1967-2M-Al-second-order",
  "Source": "R. L. Maier, \"2M interference filters for the ultraviolet,\" Thin Solid Films 1, 31-37 (1967). (DOI not printed in the paper) - Fig. 6 formula and caption (p. 36): L'1 = 3.825 L, L'2 = 3.71 L, L'3 = 2.816 L",
  "Notes": "Same T-optimized 2M aluminium filter operated in second order of interference (spacer +1/2 wave) to narrow the band and suppress the secondary leak near 2900 A\nSpecification: Maximum transmittance at 2537 A in second order, with the secondary transmittance band near 2900 A suppressed.\nReported: Computed (Fig. 6, p. 36): Tmax = 0.80 at ~2537 A, bandwidth (FWHM) 64 A; secondary band near 2900 A considerably suppressed (residual bump T ~0.1 near 2700-2800 A in the figure); T ~0 from ~3000 to ~5000 A; the first-order band only slightly evident near 5500 A (T rising to ~0.05 at 5500 A). Second-order spacer and matching layers are the first-order values + 2 QW (each + half wave): 1.825 -> 3.825, 1.71 -> 3.71, 0.816 -> 2.816.\nNotes: Paper writes the stack from the substrate: QUARTZ (LH)^8 L'1 M L'2 M L'3 (LH)^7 AIR; reversed here (layer 1 = ThF4 next to air). 35 layers, identical to Maier1967-2M-Al-first-order except L'1 = 3.825 L, L'2 = 3.71 L, L'3 = 2.816 L. L'3 sits directly against the last L of (LH)^7 as printed (combined 3.816 QW of cryolite). Same material assumptions: constant non-absorbing n_H = 1.55 (ThF4), n_L = 1.36 (cryolite), n_s = 1.48 (quartz) - applied over 200-550 nm here although the paper quotes them only at 2537 A, so the 5500 A behaviour is only approximate; Al from the library (no data in paper). Normal incidence, semi-infinite substrate assumed. Computed only; not built.\nLayers from Fig. 6 formula and caption (p. 36): L'1 = 3.825 L, L'2 = 3.71 L, L'3 = 2.816 L; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours peak T 0.808 at 253.7 nm, FWHM 6.0 nm (Rakic Al); paper T 0.80 at 253.7 nm, FWHM 6.4 nm (computed). Aluminium is Rakic’s data, not the paper’s.",
  "Wavelength": [
    253.7
  ],
